<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Colony of Trinidad</h1><p class="st-meta">1797 CE – 1889 CE · capital Port of Spain</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">British colony on Trinidad, captured from Spain in 1797, ruled directly by the crown and transformed by Indian indentured migration after slavery ended.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/spanish-trinidad">Spanish Trinidad</a> had been thinly settled until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cedula-of-population">Cédula de Población</a> of 1783 drew French Catholic planters and their enslaved workers. In February 1797 a British force under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ralph-abercromby">Sir Ralph Abercromby</a> captured the island almost without resistance, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-amiens">Treaty of Amiens</a> in 1802 confirmed the cession.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> became an experimental crown colony with no elected assembly, run by governors such as the harsh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-picton">Thomas Picton</a> under a mix of Spanish law and British practice. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">Sugar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a> estates expanded with enslaved and then free labour. From 1845 to 1917 some 143,000 Indian indentured workers arrived, bringing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> culture and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> remained strong, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad-and-tobago-carnival">Carnival</a> took shape after emancipation.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1889 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> with struggling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobago">Tobago</a>, creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-trinidad-and-tobago">Colony of Trinidad and Tobago</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-of-spain">Port of Spain</a> remained the capital, and the oil industry, which began with commercial drilling in the early 20th century, soon reshaped the economy.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1797 CE – 1889 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><path d="M50 28 L32 56 H50"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">colony</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:95.46%;width:1.83%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1797 CE – 1889 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Port of Spain</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sir Ralph Abercromby, Thomas Picton</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, Anglicanism and Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English and Trinidadian French Creole</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/trinidad-and-tobago">Trinidad and Tobago</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>British Trinidad</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1843&amp;focus=colony-of-trinidad" aria-label="Colony of Trinidad on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2985 1780 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Colony of Trinidad lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3305 2036L4997 651M3305 2036L3312 2043M3305 2036L3305 2036"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4997 651h0M3312 2043h0M3305 2036h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3305 2036h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3305 2036h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Port of Spain. <a href="/atlas?